export default {
  "wt2-education-01": "It is widely believed that universities exist mainly to prepare students for the world of work, and some people therefore argue that institutions of higher education should focus on practical skills rather than academic theory. While I accept that employability has become increasingly important, I disagree with the view that theory should be abandoned, because practical ability and theoretical understanding are in fact mutually reinforcing. Admittedly, there are good reasons to emphasise practical skills. Employers frequently complain that graduates arrive with impressive qualifications but little hands-on experience, and courses that incorporate internships, laboratory work and project-based learning produce candidates who can contribute from their first day. An engineering graduate who cannot use industry software, or a business student unfamiliar with real markets, is of limited immediate value. In an era of fierce competition for jobs, practical competence is rightly prized. Nevertheless, academic theory provides the foundation on which practical skills are built. A doctor who has merely memorised standard procedures cannot easily diagnose a patient whose symptoms are unusual, whereas a physician grounded in physiology and pharmacology can reason from first principles. The same is true of software developers, economists and teachers: specific techniques quickly become outdated, but the capacity for critical analysis and abstract reasoning remains valuable throughout a career. Universities that abandon theory therefore risk training technicians rather than thinkers. In conclusion, although I acknowledge that universities should give practical training greater weight, I believe that discarding academic theory would be short-sighted. The most effective institutions are those that blend the two, and I therefore disagree with those who see them as competing priorities.",
  "wt2-education-02": "The question of who should pay for higher education is hotly debated in many countries. While some people believe that the state should cover the full cost of university study, others argue that students themselves ought to bear the expense. Both positions have merit, but in my view a shared financial model is the most sensible. Those in favour of government funding point out that education is a public good. A well-educated population produces doctors, engineers and teachers who benefit society as a whole, so it is reasonable for taxpayers to contribute. Moreover, free tuition widens access: capable students from poor families are not deterred by fees, and societies avoid wasting talent. Countries with heavily subsidised universities, such as Germany, enjoy high enrolment rates and strong social mobility. Opponents, however, contend that higher education largely benefits the individual. University graduates typically earn considerably more over their lifetimes than non-graduates, and it seems unfair that a cleaner's taxes should subsidise the studies of a future banker. They add that students who pay for their education value it more and complete their degrees faster, while private contributions relieve strained public budgets and allow governments to spend more on schools and hospitals. In my opinion, neither extreme is desirable. Full state funding is expensive and can encourage complacency, while leaving students to pay everything would shut many talented young people out of higher education. The fairest approach is a system of income-contingent loans, in which graduates repay their fees only once they earn above a certain threshold. In conclusion, both arguments are persuasive, yet I believe the burden should be shared between the state and the student rather than resting entirely on either side.",
  "wt2-education-03": "An increasing number of parents are choosing to educate their children at home rather than send them to conventional schools. While this approach certainly has attractions, I believe that its drawbacks, particularly in terms of social development, outweigh its benefits for the majority of children. The advantages of home schooling are undeniable. Children can learn at their own pace, and the curriculum can be tailored to their individual interests and strengths, which often produces remarkable academic results. Home-educated children are also sheltered from bullying and unhealthy peer pressure, and they develop close relationships with their families. In addition, families who travel frequently or live in remote areas may find school attendance simply impractical. On the other hand, the limitations are serious. Most parents lack the training of professional teachers, and few can match the range of subjects, laboratories and libraries that a school offers. More importantly, home-educated children miss out on daily interaction with peers of the same age, and this can impair their ability to cooperate, negotiate and form friendships. Social skills are learnt through practice rather than through textbooks, and the school playground is where most of this practice naturally occurs. Furthermore, schools teach children to cope with rules, deadlines and people they do not necessarily like, which is excellent preparation for adult life and hard to replicate at home. In conclusion, although home schooling suits particular families and children with special needs, I am convinced that for most young people the loss of socialisation and specialist teaching makes it a poorer choice than a good school. The disadvantages, in my view, clearly outweigh the advantages.",
  "wt2-education-04": "Schools constantly have to decide how to divide limited time between academic subjects and physical education. Some people argue that sport should be given a much larger share of the timetable, while others insist that traditional academic subjects must remain the priority. In my view, both are essential, and the best solution is a balanced curriculum in which academics stay central while physical activity receives a guaranteed place. There are compelling reasons to expand physical education. Regular exercise improves children's health at a time when obesity and sedentary lifestyles are growing problems, and it builds teamwork, discipline and resilience that benefit pupils in every area of life. Physical activity has also been shown to improve concentration and relieve stress, so active children often perform better in the classroom. For these reasons, schools in Japan and Finland schedule frequent breaks for exercise and still achieve excellent academic results. Nevertheless, academic subjects should not be neglected. In a highly competitive global economy, literacy, mathematics and science are the foundation of future careers, and examination results determine access to university. Cutting these subjects to make room for sport could disadvantage pupils precisely when competition is fiercest, and parents, who tend to judge schools by academic outcomes, would rightly be concerned. No school can afford to be seen as neglecting the core curriculum. In conclusion, I do not believe we must choose between sport and study. A well-designed school day should protect core academic learning while guaranteeing regular physical activity, since healthy, energetic students are also better learners. In my opinion, this balance serves everyone best.",
  "wt2-education-05": "In many countries, it has become normal for young people to leave their hometowns and move to large cities in order to attend university. In my opinion, this is on balance a positive development, even though it creates genuine problems for the communities they leave behind. The benefits for the students themselves are obvious. Major cities generally host the best universities, with superior libraries, laboratories and teaching staff, and they offer a far wider range of courses. Studying in a large city also exposes young people to diverse cultures, ideas and professional networks, which broadens their horizons and gives them a head start in the job market. Moreover, living away from home teaches independence, budgeting and self-reliance, qualities that employers value highly. There are, admittedly, negative consequences. Hometowns, especially in rural areas, lose many of their most talented young people, a process often described as a brain drain, and the high cost of living in cities can place a heavy financial burden on students and their families. Some young people also struggle with loneliness and culture shock when they first arrive. These difficulties, however, are usually temporary and can be managed with proper support from universities and families. For society as a whole, concentrating talent in cities helps drive innovation and economic growth, which ultimately benefits the entire country, including the regions that students came from. In conclusion, although rural communities suffer from the departure of their young people, I believe that the educational and personal advantages for individuals, together with the wider economic benefits, make this a positive development on balance.",
  "wt2-environment-01": "It is sometimes claimed that ordinary citizens can do almost nothing to protect the environment and that only governments and large corporations can bring about real change. I strongly disagree with this view, because individual actions, multiplied across millions of people, exert enormous influence, although I accept that state intervention is also essential. To begin with, collective individual behaviour shapes both pollution levels and corporate conduct. If millions of people choose to cycle or use public transport, recycle waste, reduce meat consumption and switch to energy-efficient appliances, the cumulative reduction in emissions is considerable. Consumer choices also drive business behaviour: companies produce sustainable goods because shoppers demand them, as the growth of organic food and electric vehicles demonstrates. Firms respond to markets, and markets are made up of individuals. That said, governments and companies clearly have powers that individuals lack. Only states can legislate, impose carbon taxes, ban harmful products and fund large-scale renewable energy projects, and only businesses can redesign their supply chains. Expecting individuals to solve climate change through personal sacrifice alone would be unrealistic, and governments must create the conditions, such as affordable public transport and safe cycling routes, that make green choices easy. The two levels of action are therefore complementary rather than opposed. Laws are only as effective as the public's willingness to comply, and companies respond to the pressure that informed consumers apply, so neither can succeed alone. In conclusion, I reject the claim that citizens are powerless. Individuals, governments and businesses all have vital roles, and lasting environmental progress requires determined action at every level.",
  "wt2-environment-02": "International travel is frequently criticised for its environmental impact, and some people suggest that tourists should cut back on flying and explore their own countries instead. While I agree that tourism contributes to pollution, I believe that discouraging foreign travel would cause more harm than good, and that the wiser course is to make tourism more sustainable. It is true that long-haul travel is damaging. Aeroplanes emit large quantities of carbon dioxide, popular destinations suffer from overcrowding and waste, and fragile ecosystems, such as coral reefs and mountain regions, are degraded by mass tourism. If people confined their holidays to their own countries, emissions from aviation would fall sharply and local businesses would gain custom. For a short-haul traveller, a domestic break can be just as rewarding as a foreign one. Nevertheless, international tourism brings benefits that should not be sacrificed lightly. For many developing countries, tourism is a vital source of income and employment, and it often funds the conservation of wildlife and heritage sites that would otherwise be neglected. Travel also promotes cultural understanding and reduces prejudice by allowing people to experience other ways of life first-hand. Moreover, aviation accounts for only a small fraction of global emissions compared with industry and agriculture, so restricting holidays alone would not solve the problem. The answer is not to stop travelling but to travel responsibly: choosing nearer destinations, flying less frequently, staying longer and supporting eco-friendly operators. In conclusion, while the environmental cost of international travel is real, I believe that discouraging it is misguided. Encouraging responsible, low-impact tourism offers a far better balance between protecting the planet and preserving the benefits of travel.",
  "wt2-environment-03": "Single-use plastic products, such as bags, bottles and straws, have become a target of environmental policy worldwide, and many governments plan to ban them. In my view, such bans bring substantial benefits, but they also involve costs that should be carefully considered before the policy is applied too widely. The advantages of banning single-use plastics are considerable. These products are a major source of pollution: they choke rivers and oceans, entangle and kill marine wildlife, and take centuries to decompose in landfill sites. A ban removes this waste at its source and encourages manufacturers to develop biodegradable or reusable alternatives. It also raises public awareness, as consumers begin to think about packaging when they shop, and countries that have introduced bans, such as Kenya, have seen dramatic falls in plastic litter. However, there are disadvantages too. Plastic is cheap, light and hygienic, and alternatives such as paper or bamboo are often more expensive, which raises costs for businesses and, ultimately, for consumers. In the food and medical sectors, single-use packaging protects hygiene, and poorly designed substitutes can be even worse for the environment because they consume more water or energy to produce. Enforcement also poses difficulties, particularly in countries with limited resources, where illegal plastic may simply flood in from elsewhere. These problems suggest that a complete and immediate ban may be unrealistic. Targeted restrictions, exemptions for essential uses and investment in alternatives would be more effective. In conclusion, the environmental benefits of banning single-use plastics clearly outweigh the drawbacks in most cases, but governments should phase the ban in gradually and ensure that practical alternatives exist, so that the policy protects the planet without imposing unacceptable costs.",
  "wt2-environment-04": "Some environmentalists argue that increasing the price of fuel and other energy sources is the single most effective way of tackling environmental problems. While I accept that higher prices can discourage consumption, I disagree that this is the best solution, because it is unfair to the poor and needs to be combined with other measures. There is no doubt that price signals work. When fuel becomes expensive, people drive less, choose smaller and more efficient cars, and switch to public transport, while industries invest in energy-saving technology. Higher energy costs also make renewable sources such as solar and wind power more competitive, accelerating the transition away from fossil fuels. In this sense, taxation is a powerful and immediate tool. Nevertheless, there are serious drawbacks. Raising energy prices hits low-income households hardest, since they spend a larger share of their income on heating and transport, yet they can least afford electric cars or solar panels. The resulting public anger can undermine support for environmental policy altogether, as seen in recent protests against fuel taxes across Europe. Moreover, demand for energy is relatively inelastic in the short term, so price rises may cause hardship without quickly reducing consumption. The most effective approach therefore combines several tools: modest green taxes, generous subsidies for public transport and renewable energy, strict emission regulations and public education campaigns. Together these create both the incentive and the ability to change behaviour. In conclusion, although raising energy prices has a part to play, I reject the claim that it is the most effective solution. A balanced package of measures, designed to protect vulnerable households, is far more likely to succeed.",
  "wt2-environment-05": "Fresh water is becoming dangerously scarce in many parts of the world, threatening agriculture, industry and public health. This problem has several root causes, but it can be tackled through a combination of technological, economic and educational measures. The causes of water shortages are diverse. Rapid population growth and urbanisation increase demand at a pace that infrastructure cannot match, while climate change brings more frequent droughts and shrinking glaciers that once fed major rivers. Pollution is another culprit: industrial waste, agricultural chemicals and untreated sewage contaminate rivers and aquifers, making vast quantities of water unusable. Finally, water is often wasted through outdated irrigation methods, leaking pipes and the absence of pricing that reflects its true value, so farmers and households have little incentive to conserve. Fortunately, workable solutions exist. Governments can invest in modern drip irrigation and water recycling, build desalination plants for coastal cities, and repair leaky distribution networks, measures that can recover enormous volumes of water. Introducing fair water pricing and metering encourages users to save, while stricter regulations can prevent factories and farms from polluting supplies. Rainwater harvesting and the protection of wetlands and forests also help maintain the natural water cycle. Public education is equally important. Campaigns that teach children and adults to take shorter showers, fix dripping taps and avoid wasting water can change habits at little cost, and international cooperation is needed to share technology and expertise with the poorest countries, which are often hit hardest by shortages. In conclusion, water scarcity is driven by growing demand, climate change and pollution, but it is far from inevitable. With investment in infrastructure, sensible pricing, tougher pollution controls and public awareness, governments can secure clean water for future generations.",
  "wt2-technology-01": "It is often argued that the spread of computers and smartphones is damaging young people's reading and writing abilities. I believe this concern is partly justified, since heavy screen use encourages superficial habits, but I would also argue that technology itself is not the enemy: much depends on how it is used. There is clear evidence that digital habits can weaken literacy. Young people increasingly skim short texts and social media posts rather than reading books at length, so their attention spans shrink and their vocabulary stagnates. Writing, too, is affected: text messages and online posts are full of abbreviations and emojis, and spell-checkers mean that few teenagers can spell correctly without assistance. A student who never writes more than a few sentences at a time will find essay writing at university genuinely difficult. On the other hand, technology offers remarkable opportunities for literacy. E-readers and educational apps give children access to an almost unlimited supply of books, often at lower cost than paper copies, and word processors with grammar tools allow young writers to draft, revise and improve their work far more easily than with pen and paper. Many students read more, not less, because they can follow their interests online. The problem, therefore, lies in how devices are used rather than in the devices themselves. Schools and parents have a crucial role in this: setting limits on recreational screen time, encouraging sustained reading and teaching children that good writing requires effort regardless of the medium. In conclusion, I agree that careless use of computers and smartphones can harm reading and writing, but I do not believe this effect is inevitable. With sensible guidance, digital technology can enhance, rather than undermine, young people's literacy.",
  "wt2-technology-02": "Advances in robotics and artificial intelligence have led many experts to predict that machines will eventually replace human workers in most jobs. While this prospect alarms many people, I believe it is on balance a positive development, provided that societies manage the transition wisely. The benefits of automation are considerable. Robots can perform dangerous, dirty and repetitive tasks far more safely than people, from mining and manufacturing to handling toxic waste, saving thousands of lives every year. Automation also raises productivity and lowers costs, which makes goods and services cheaper and can raise living standards. Furthermore, history suggests that new technologies create as many jobs as they destroy: the internet destroyed some occupations but created entirely new industries such as e-commerce and app development. Nevertheless, the risks should not be underestimated. If machines take over most employment, millions of people could face long-term unemployment, and the resulting loss of income and purpose can fuel poverty, inequality and social unrest. Older workers, in particular, may struggle to retrain, and the gap between highly skilled technology workers and everyone else could widen dramatically. There is also a danger that human qualities such as empathy and judgement would be undervalued if automation were pursued for its own sake. These dangers, however, are not inevitable. Governments can invest heavily in education and retraining, provide income support during the transition, and use the productivity gains from automation to fund better public services. In conclusion, the replacement of human workers by machines is a development with real risks, but it promises enormous benefits if we prepare for it. On balance, I view it as positive, because automation frees people from drudgery and creates new opportunities, as long as the gains are shared fairly.",
  "wt2-technology-03": "Some people claim that modern technology has made life more complicated rather than simpler. While I concede that constant connectivity brings new pressures, I largely disagree, because for most people technology has removed far more burdens than it has created. Consider the ordinary tasks that technology has transformed. Banking, shopping, booking travel and paying bills can now be completed online in minutes, instead of requiring visits to several offices during working hours. Navigation apps eliminate the stress of getting lost, instant messaging lets families keep in touch across continents, and search engines put the world's knowledge at our fingertips. Household appliances such as dishwashers and washing machines have freed people from hours of domestic labour, while telemedicine allows patients to consult doctors without travelling. It is true that technology introduces new complications. Emails and notifications follow us everywhere, blurring the boundary between work and leisure, and we must constantly update devices, manage passwords and learn new interfaces. Social media, in particular, can overwhelm users with information and comparison, creating anxiety that previous generations never experienced. For the elderly or the less educated, digital services can even be a barrier, since banks and government offices increasingly expect online contact. These drawbacks, however, are largely matters of design and self-discipline rather than inevitable consequences. We can switch off notifications, set aside offline time and demand that services remain accessible to everyone. In conclusion, although modern technology certainly adds some complexity to daily life, I believe its ability to simplify communication, shopping, travel and work is far greater. On balance, technology has made our lives easier, and it is up to us to use it sensibly.",
  "wt2-technology-04": "Thanks to the internet and mobile technology, a growing number of employees now work from home instead of commuting to an office. In my view, the advantages of this arrangement outweigh the disadvantages for most workers, although companies need to manage it carefully. The benefits for employees are substantial. Above all, remote work eliminates commuting, saving hours of travel time and considerable money each week, and reducing stress and traffic pollution at the same time. It also offers flexibility, allowing parents to balance work with childcare and people in remote areas to access jobs that would otherwise be out of reach. Employees often report that they are more productive at home, free from office distractions, and employers benefit from lower overheads and a wider talent pool. Admittedly, working from home has drawbacks. Some employees feel isolated, missing the social contact and informal conversations that spark creativity and build team spirit. Others find it hard to switch off, so that work spills into evenings and weekends, and domestic interruptions can be a problem, particularly for those with young children. There is also a risk that career progression slows for remote workers, who are less visible to their managers than colleagues in the office. These difficulties, however, can largely be overcome with good management: clear boundaries, regular video meetings, occasional office days and fair promotion criteria. A hybrid model, in which employees work from home for part of the week and attend the office for the rest, seems to capture the best of both worlds. In conclusion, while home working has challenges, I believe the savings in time, cost and stress, together with greater flexibility, mean that its advantages outweigh its disadvantages for most workers and employers.",
  "wt2-technology-05": "Scientific research is financed and conducted by both governments and private companies, and there is considerable debate about which of the two should take the lead. Some people argue that governments should control all research, while others defend the role of the private sector. In my opinion, neither side should dominate, and a partnership between the two produces the best results. Those who favour government control make a strong case. State-funded research is guided by public needs rather than profit, which is why vaccines, disease prevention and climate science are largely publicly supported, and its results are usually published openly so that everyone can benefit. Private companies, by contrast, may concentrate on whatever is most profitable and keep their findings secret behind patents, which can delay progress and restrict access to life-saving medicines. However, the private sector brings strengths that governments often lack. Companies are efficient, move quickly and are willing to invest in risky but promising ideas, and the commercial pressure to succeed encourages innovation. Much of the technology we take for granted, from smartphones to search engines, was developed by private firms, and competition keeps prices down. Governments, by contrast, can be slow and bureaucratic, and research controlled entirely by the state may be distorted by political priorities or censorship. The sensible solution is a division of labour: governments should fund and oversee basic research and regulate safety and ethics, while private companies develop and commercialise products, with transparency requirements ensuring that essential discoveries are shared. In conclusion, although government control guarantees that research serves the public interest, private companies drive innovation efficiently. I therefore believe that a well-regulated partnership is far better than allowing either sector to dominate.",
  "wt2-health-01": "There is ongoing debate about who should take responsibility for public health. Some people believe that governments should guarantee that citizens live healthy lives, whereas others insist that health is a personal matter. In my opinion, health is a shared responsibility in which the state provides the framework and individuals make the final choices. Governments clearly have a crucial role. They are the only bodies able to fund hospitals, provide clean water, run vaccination programmes and regulate dangerous products, and public health measures such as smoking bans and food labelling have saved countless lives. Without state action, preventive services would be unaffordable for many people, and diseases that could easily be contained by vaccination would spread. The recent pandemic demonstrated beyond doubt that governments must coordinate health policy on a large scale. Nevertheless, individuals cannot simply hand over responsibility for their health to the state. Whether a person exercises, eats a balanced diet, sleeps enough and avoids smoking is a daily personal choice that no government can enforce, and healthy habits are shaped in the home and the workplace rather than in clinics. Moreover, if people expect the state to repair every consequence of unhealthy living, healthcare costs will spiral, and individuals will have little incentive to change their behaviour. The two positions are not mutually exclusive. Governments can make healthy choices easy through education, taxation and the design of cities, while citizens must respond by looking after themselves. In conclusion, both governments and individuals have essential roles in maintaining health. I believe the best approach is one of partnership, in which the state creates the conditions for health and individuals take personal responsibility for their daily choices.",
  "wt2-health-02": "Obesity has become one of the most serious public health problems of our time, and the number of overweight people is rising rapidly in many countries. This essay will examine the main causes of this trend and suggest practical solutions. The causes are rooted in modern lifestyles. First, diets have changed dramatically: processed foods, takeaways and sugary drinks are cheap, convenient and heavily advertised, while traditional home cooking has declined. Second, physical activity has fallen, since many people spend their working day sitting at desks and their leisure time watching screens, and cars have replaced walking for most short journeys. Finally, larger portion sizes and the stress of busy modern life, which often leads to emotional eating, make it harder to maintain a healthy weight. Governments can take effective action. Clear nutritional labelling and restrictions on advertising junk food to children help people make informed choices, while taxes on sugary drinks, such as those introduced in Britain and Mexico, have been shown to reduce consumption. Subsidising fresh fruit and vegetables would make healthy food affordable, and building safe cycle paths, parks and affordable sports facilities would encourage people to be active. Schools and families also have a part to play. Cookery lessons and daily physical education shape children's habits for life, parental example is powerful, and workplace wellness programmes can help adults stay active. These measures are most effective when they work together. In conclusion, the obesity epidemic is caused by unhealthy diets, sedentary lifestyles and modern stress, but it can be reversed through a combination of regulation, education and better urban design. Tackling it requires action from governments, schools, families and individuals alike.",
  "wt2-health-03": "Some people argue that building more sports facilities is the best way to improve public health, while others doubt that such investment would achieve much. In my view, new facilities are helpful, but they are not the whole answer, and a broader approach is required. The case for sports facilities is straightforward. Free or cheap gyms, swimming pools and football pitches give people a convenient place to exercise, and convenient exercise is exercise that actually happens. Facilities also create community hubs where people can meet, which supports mental as well as physical health. In countries such as Australia, public investment in sports grounds is associated with high levels of participation, and children who have playgrounds nearby are far more likely to be active than those who do not. However, it would be naive to expect buildings alone to transform public health. Many people already have access to parks or gyms but never use them, because they lack motivation, time or knowledge of how to exercise safely. Obesity is driven as much by diet as by inactivity, so no number of sports centres will solve a problem caused largely by junk food. Moreover, building facilities is expensive, and the money might be better spent on public health campaigns, school meals or preventive medicine. The most effective strategy therefore combines several elements: modest investment in facilities, education about diet and exercise, compulsory physical education in schools, and urban planning that makes walking and cycling easy. In conclusion, although sports facilities can encourage activity and strengthen communities, I believe they are only one part of an effective public health policy. Calling them the best solution overstates their power; a combination of measures will always achieve more.",
  "wt2-health-04": "A growing number of people are turning to alternative medicines, such as herbal remedies, acupuncture and homeopathy, instead of visiting a doctor. Although some of these therapies can be beneficial, I believe that using them as a substitute for conventional medicine is generally a negative development. The main danger is delay. When someone relies on alternative treatment for a serious condition such as diabetes or cancer, diagnosis is postponed, and by the time they see a doctor the illness may be far more advanced and harder to treat. Some alternative products are also unsafe: herbal remedies can interact dangerously with prescription drugs, and unregulated supplements have caused serious organ damage. Money spent on ineffective treatments is another concern, since many therapies have little scientific evidence behind them and simply drain patients' savings. That is not to say alternative medicine has no value. Certain therapies, such as massage, meditation and physiotherapy-style techniques, genuinely relieve pain and stress, and patients often value the time and attention that alternative practitioners give them. When used alongside conventional treatment, with the knowledge of a qualified doctor, these approaches can improve quality of life. There is also a cultural dimension: many people trust traditional remedies that have been used in their families for generations. The key issue is substitution versus complement. Alternative medicine is harmful when it replaces proper medical care, but genuinely beneficial when it supplements it. In conclusion, while alternative therapies can play a useful supporting role, I regard the growing tendency to use them instead of consulting a doctor as a negative development, because it endangers health, delays treatment and often wastes money. Patients should be encouraged to combine the best of both worlds under medical guidance.",
  "wt2-health-05": "Obesity and diet-related diseases are rising, and many people argue that advertising for unhealthy food and sugary drinks should be banned in the interests of public health. While I agree that such advertising is harmful and should be restricted, I do not believe a total ban alone would solve the problem. There is strong evidence that junk food advertising influences consumption, particularly among children, who cannot easily distinguish between entertainment and marketing. Cartoon characters, bright colours and free toys make unhealthy products irresistible, and children then pressure their parents to buy them. Countries that have restricted advertising to children, such as the United Kingdom, which banned junk food advertisements on television before nine o'clock in the evening, have seen encouraging changes in purchasing patterns. Restricting such advertising is therefore a legitimate public health measure, just as tobacco advertising was banned decades ago. On the other hand, advertising is only one factor driving poor diets, and banning it would not remove the underlying causes, such as the low cost of processed food, the limited availability of fresh produce in poor areas and the lack of cooking skills. A total ban would also raise concerns about commercial freedom, since adults can reasonably argue that they should be allowed to see information about products and make their own choices. Moreover, companies would simply redirect their marketing to social media and streaming platforms, which are harder to regulate. The most effective approach combines several measures: strict limits on advertising aimed at children, clear labelling, taxes on sugary products and education about healthy eating. In conclusion, I strongly support restrictions on advertising for unhealthy food and drinks, especially where children are concerned, but I doubt that a complete ban is either fair or sufficient on its own. It should be one part of a wider strategy to improve public health.",
  "wt2-society-01": "The widening gap between the rich and the poor is one of the most pressing social problems of our time. This essay will discuss the problems that growing inequality creates and suggest measures that governments can take to address them. Economic inequality produces a range of social ills. When the gap widens, social mobility declines, because wealthy families can buy the best education, housing and connections for their children, while the poor remain trapped in deprived areas with under-funded schools. Inequality also breeds crime, resentment and political instability, as people who feel excluded from prosperity lose trust in institutions. Public health suffers too: research consistently shows that more unequal societies have higher rates of mental illness, obesity and violence, and the strain on public services grows as the rich buy private alternatives and lose interest in funding schools and hospitals. Governments can narrow the gap through several policies. Progressive taxation, in which the wealthy pay a higher proportion of their income, funds better schools, healthcare and social housing, while a decent minimum wage and stronger workers' rights raise the incomes of the poorest. Investment in education and vocational training is especially important, since it gives disadvantaged children the skills to compete, and affordable childcare allows parents to work rather than depend on welfare. Ultimately, reducing inequality is not only a matter of fairness; it benefits everyone, because more equal societies are healthier, safer and more productive. In conclusion, widening inequality causes serious social, economic and health problems, from falling mobility to rising crime. However, these problems are not inevitable: through fair taxation, quality public services and educational opportunity, governments can create a fairer society in which prosperity is shared.",
  "wt2-society-02": "In many countries, falling birth rates and longer life expectancy mean that the population is ageing rapidly. Although an older society brings certain benefits, I believe that, on balance, this is a negative development that will place severe strain on economies and public services unless it is managed carefully. The negative consequences are well documented. A shrinking workforce means fewer taxpayers supporting a growing number of pensioners, so pension systems come under pressure and governments must either raise taxes, cut benefits or increase debt. Health and social care costs rise sharply, since older people need more hospital treatment and long-term care, and the burden often falls on families, particularly women, who give up work to care for elderly relatives. A shortage of younger workers can also slow economic growth and reduce innovation. That said, ageing populations are not purely a burden. Many older people remain active and productive, contributing valuable experience to the workforce, and they support their families financially and practically, often helping with childcare. The so-called silver economy, which serves the needs of older consumers, creates jobs, and societies with large elderly populations tend to be stable and peaceful. Furthermore, advances in medicine mean that many people remain healthy well into old age. Whether the balance is positive or negative depends largely on policy. Raising the retirement age gradually, encouraging the immigration of working-age people, investing in automation and promoting healthy ageing can all reduce the burden on younger generations. In conclusion, although older societies enjoy some advantages, the economic and social costs of an ageing population outweigh them. I therefore regard it as a negative development, though one that can be managed successfully through timely and sensible policies.",
  "wt2-society-03": "More and more people, especially in large cities, are choosing to live alone, a trend that was almost unknown a few decades ago. While living alone offers genuine freedom and independence, I believe that, on balance, this is a negative development because of its effects on loneliness and community life. The attractions of living alone are easy to understand. It offers complete independence, privacy and control over one's own space and schedule, which is particularly appealing to young professionals who value their autonomy. Living alone also allows people to focus on their careers and personal development without domestic compromise, and for many it is simply the practical consequence of moving to a city for work. In some cases, it can even build self-reliance, since people must manage their finances, cooking and household repairs for themselves. However, the drawbacks are serious. Living alone is strongly associated with loneliness, and research links loneliness to depression, anxiety and even poor physical health. Without flatmates or family members around, people have fewer everyday social interactions, and in large anonymous cities it is easy to spend whole days speaking to almost no one. The trend also contributes to the decline of community life, since neighbourhoods occupied by single-person households become less lively, and it is environmentally inefficient, because more housing and energy are needed per person. While living alone is a personal choice that suits some people, societies need to respond, for example by building shared housing and community spaces that encourage connection. In conclusion, although living alone gives individuals freedom and privacy, the resulting isolation and the weakening of communities lead me to regard it as a negative development. Planners should promote housing models that combine independence with social connection.",
  "wt2-society-04": "Reducing crime is a constant priority for governments, but there is disagreement about the best method. Some people believe that longer prison sentences are the answer, while others argue that education and community service are more effective. In my opinion, both approaches have merits, but the best results come from a combination in which rehabilitation is given greater emphasis. The case for longer sentences rests on deterrence and public safety. If offenders know that they will face severe punishment, they may think twice before committing crimes, and while they are in prison they cannot harm the public. Long sentences also satisfy the public's demand for justice and express society's condemnation of serious wrongdoing, which many victims and their families regard as essential. Nevertheless, prison is an expensive and often ineffective remedy. It costs a great deal to keep someone locked up, and prisons frequently function as schools of crime, where offenders learn new techniques and leave more dangerous than they entered, as high rates of reoffending demonstrate. By contrast, education and community service tackle the root causes of crime, such as poor literacy, unemployment and lack of direction, and allow offenders to make amends while remaining productive members of society. Schemes that teach skills and provide mentoring have been shown to cut reoffending dramatically. The sensible approach is to use each tool where it works best: prison for violent and repeat offenders, and education, training and community sentences for minor and first-time offenders. In conclusion, although long prison sentences have a role in protecting the public and punishing serious crime, I believe that education and community service are more effective in the long term, and that a balanced system centred on rehabilitation serves society better than a purely punitive one.",
  "wt2-society-05": "Some people argue that volunteering should be made compulsory for all young people, so that every teenager contributes to the community. While I recognise the potential benefits of requiring a period of service, I believe that forcing young people to volunteer is the wrong approach, and I only partly agree with this proposal. There is much to be said for making community service a requirement. Young people would develop empathy, responsibility and practical skills, and society would benefit from the enormous amount of work done in care homes, charities and environmental projects. Compulsory schemes in countries such as Germany and Israel, where young people perform national service, have fostered a strong sense of civic duty, and many participants report that the experience changed their outlook on life. However, compulsory volunteering is almost a contradiction in terms. Volunteering means offering one's time freely, and work that is demanded is unlikely to inspire genuine commitment; indeed, resentful participants may do more harm than good and turn away from community service for life. A legal requirement would also be difficult to enforce and unfair, since some young people must work to support their families, while others have caring responsibilities or are preparing for demanding examinations. A better alternative is to integrate service into education. Schools and universities can offer well-organised volunteering schemes, recognise participation with certificates and awards, and make a short service project part of the curriculum, which encourages participation without compulsion. In conclusion, although requiring young people to volunteer would bring social benefits and develop valuable qualities, I believe that coercion undermines the very spirit of volunteering. I therefore disagree with making it compulsory and favour encouraging service through education and recognition instead.",
  "wt2-work-01": "The question of working hours divides employers and employees. Some people believe that workers should be free to choose their own schedules, while others maintain that fixed hours are better for everyone. In my opinion, flexibility is highly desirable, but it works best within a framework of agreed core hours. The advantages of flexible working are considerable. When employees can choose when to start and finish, they can arrange their day around family responsibilities, exercise or simply their own natural rhythms, and research shows that this makes people more productive and less stressed. Flexible hours also allow companies to cover a longer span of the day, since some employees may choose to work early and others late, and they make jobs attractive to parents and carers who would otherwise be unable to work at all. Nevertheless, fixed schedules have real strengths. In jobs that serve customers, such as retail, banking and healthcare, staff must be present at predictable times, and rigid timetables make coordination and teamwork straightforward. Fixed hours also create a clear boundary between work and private life, which flexible arrangements can blur to the point where employees feel that they are always on duty. They also treat everyone equally, avoiding accusations of favouritism when managers decide who may work from home or finish early. The best compromise is flexibility within limits. Many organisations successfully use flexitime, in which employees choose their hours around a core period, such as ten until three, when everyone must be present for meetings and collaboration. In conclusion, both arrangements have merits, but I believe that giving employees freedom to choose their hours, within an agreed core, offers the best balance between individual well-being and the needs of the organisation.",
  "wt2-work-02": "Because people are living longer and healthier lives, many governments are raising the official retirement age. While I understand the financial logic behind this policy, I believe it is only partly justified, and it must be applied with flexibility to avoid unfairness. The case for raising the retirement age is strong. Longer life expectancy means that pension systems face a growing number of pensioners and a shrinking number of contributors, so delaying retirement helps keep pensions affordable without raising taxes. Many people in their late sixties and seventies remain fit, experienced and willing to work, and society benefits from their skills, while older workers themselves gain income, purpose and social contact. Forcing healthy people to retire at sixty-five when they could work for another decade wastes valuable human capital. However, the policy is unfair if applied rigidly. People in manual occupations, such as construction and nursing, often have shorter working lives because their bodies wear out, yet life expectancy is not the same for everyone: wealthier white-collar workers generally live longer than poorer manual workers, so raising the retirement age can mean that the poorest groups receive their pensions for the shortest time. Older employees also struggle to find new jobs if they are made redundant, and keeping older workers in post can slow promotion for younger people at a time when youth unemployment is already high. The solution is flexibility: a gradual increase linked to health and occupation, with earlier retirement for physically demanding jobs and part-time options for those who want to ease into retirement. In conclusion, raising the retirement age is a sensible response to longer life expectancy, and I largely agree with it, but only if it is phased in gradually and adapted to the realities of different jobs and different workers.",
  "wt2-work-03": "When choosing a career, people weigh many factors, and one of the oldest debates concerns the relative importance of job satisfaction and salary. While a good income provides security and comfort, I strongly believe that job satisfaction matters more for long-term happiness and well-being. The importance of enjoying one's work can hardly be overstated. Most adults spend roughly a third of their waking lives at work, so a job that is dull, stressful or meaningless poisons a huge part of each day, no matter how well it is paid. Workers who find their jobs meaningful are more motivated, more productive and less likely to suffer from burnout and depression, and research on happiness consistently finds that purpose and relationships at work matter far more to well-being than income beyond a certain point. A teacher or nurse who loves the job can be far happier than a banker who dreads Monday morning. Nevertheless, salary should not be dismissed. Money meets basic needs, provides security against illness and unemployment, and offers choices, such as housing, education and travel, that themselves contribute to happiness. Low pay can cause constant worry and force people to take second jobs, leaving no energy for family or hobbies, and in an expensive city a poorly paid but enjoyable job may simply be unaffordable. The difficulty is that we often only discover how much a salary matters when it is missing. In my view, the ideal is a job that offers enough income to live comfortably and work that is genuinely rewarding, and where the two conflict, satisfaction should usually win. In conclusion, although a decent salary is important and should not be sacrificed without thought, I believe job satisfaction is the more important of the two, because it affects our health, motivation and happiness every single day.",
  "wt2-work-04": "The days when people stayed with one employer for life are long gone, and many young people now change jobs several times during their careers. In my opinion, the advantages of this trend clearly outweigh the disadvantages, particularly in the early stages of a working life. Frequent job changes offer young workers valuable opportunities. Each new position brings different skills, industries and colleagues, so job-hoppers quickly build a broad and adaptable skillset, which is exactly what employers increasingly demand in a fast-changing economy. Changing jobs is also the surest route to higher pay, since salaries typically jump when people move rather than when they stay, and it allows young people to discover what they genuinely enjoy before settling down. Those who have worked in several environments also develop resilience, communication skills and a professional network that serves them for life. There are, of course, drawbacks. Employers may view a very short employment history as a sign of unreliability, and frequent moves mean that workers rarely gain the deep expertise or seniority that come from staying in one place, which can slow long-term progression. Pension contributions and other benefits are often disrupted, and each change brings the stress of interviews, probation periods and adapting to new colleagues, while constant restlessness can prevent the sense of belonging that stable employment provides. These disadvantages, however, are most serious when moves are frequent and aimless. Young people who change jobs deliberately, learning something from each position, gain far more than they lose. In conclusion, although job-hopping carries risks such as instability and shallow experience, I believe its advantages, in terms of skills, earnings and self-knowledge, outweigh its disadvantages for young people, as long as each move is purposeful and they eventually commit to a path.",
  "wt2-work-05": "Some people argue that those who perform manual jobs should be paid as much as those in professional occupations such as law, medicine and engineering. While I believe manual workers are often undervalued and deserve better pay and respect, I disagree that the two groups should receive equal salaries. It is true that manual workers are frequently taken for granted. Builders, cleaners, refuse collectors and farm workers perform essential tasks that keep society functioning, often in physically demanding and hazardous conditions, yet their wages are typically far lower than those of office workers. In many countries, nurses and construction workers earn a fraction of what bankers and consultants earn, despite contributing more directly to people's daily lives. Recognising the value of these workers, through fairer pay and better conditions, is long overdue. Nevertheless, equal pay would ignore economic reality. Professional jobs usually require many years of education and training, carry heavy responsibility and involve specialised knowledge that takes a decade to acquire, and salaries partly reflect this investment and the scarcity of qualified candidates. A surgeon who makes life-or-death decisions, or a lawyer whose advice can decide a company's future, reasonably commands higher pay, and market forces set wages according to supply and demand rather than moral worth. If salaries were equalised by law, young people would have little incentive to spend years studying medicine or engineering, and society would suffer a shortage of professionals. The answer is not to equalise pay but to raise the minimum standards for manual work: decent wages, safety, pensions and dignity. In conclusion, while manual workers deserve higher pay and greater respect than they currently receive, I disagree that they should be paid as much as professionals, since education, responsibility and market forces justify differences, and the goal should be fairer pay for all rather than artificial equality."
};
